{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0191U4LzKmntn3GpjvarTuyx","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"DFT PBE atomic oxygen binding energy Cu(111) fcc hollow eV relative to gas-phase O atom\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Multiple Adsorption of CO Molecules on Transition Metal Substitutional Impurities in Copper Surfaces\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.07858\"},{\"title\":\"CO adsorption on Cu(111) and Cu(001) surfaces: improving site preference in DFT calculations\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/0408394\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2505.12914\"},{\"title\":\"First principles study of oxidation resistance of atomically flat Cu(111) surface\",\"url\":\"https://archive.aps.org/mar/2022/g47/5\"},{\"title\":\"s11433 012 4868 5\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11433-012-4868-5.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Structural and electronic properties of atomic oxygen adsorption on Cu(111) surface: A first-principles investigation\",\"url\":\"https://bvbvf1.bib-bvb.de/vufind/PrimoRecord/cdi_chongqing_primary_44769476\"},{\"title\":\"par.nsf.gov\",\"url\":\"https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10320492\"},{\"title\":\"P 46 a11 6122919\",\"url\":\"https://accms.mobility.niche.tohoku.ac.jp/ACCMS-11/POSTER/P-46_a11_6122919.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"PhysRevB 73 165424\",\"url\":\"https://condensed.physics.usyd.edu.au/old_website/papers_aloy/PhysRevB_73_165424.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"First principles study of oxidation resistance of atomically flat Cu(111) surface\",\"url\":\"https://https-meetings--archive-aps-org-443.webvpn1.xju.edu.cn/mar/2022/g47/5/\"}]\n\nThe search turned up one clear PBE result and one conflicting value, so the number depends on the source.\n\n- **Best match (PBE, VASP):** An arXiv paper on O on Cu(111) reports the fcc hollow site at -5.000 eV and the hcp hollow at -4.905 eV. The exchange-correlation energy was described using the GGA-based PBE functional. The energy reference is an isolated oxygen atom in the gas phase. In that convention, negative values mean binding is favorable. The fcc and hcp threefold hollow sites are the energetically preferred binding sites for O*.\n- **Conflicting value:** A Tohoku poster reports adsorption energies of -6.37 eV (fcc) and -6.26 eV (hcp) on Cu(111). The calculated adsorption energies were -6.37 eV and -6.26 eV for the fcc and hcp hollow sites, respectively. The poster names a PAW method but does not state the functional, and its reference state is not clear. The gap of about 1.4 eV from the PBE value suggests a different reference or setup, so I would not treat it as a PBE benchmark without checking the original paper.\n- **Other support:** A study of atomic O on Cu(111) also found the fcc-hollow site is the most stable site for oxygen adsorption. That result does not give an energy value or functional.\n\nBottom line: the most direct PBE number for fcc-hollow O on Cu(111), referenced to a gas-phase O atom, is about -5.0 eV. The -6.37 eV figure is probably not comparable without checking its setup.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e8134b61-5a82-4eb5-8ce2-5d1136f1766d","uuid":"56691054-8d09-4607-abdf-78f823591090","timestamp":"2026-11-01T02:52:02.944Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"DFT PBE atomic oxygen binding energy Cu(111) fcc hollow eV relative to gas-phase O atom","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01RrXgYRaszyEzcZTu7xLrJR","content":[{"title":"Multiple Adsorption of CO Molecules on Transition Metal Substitutional Impurities in Copper Surfaces","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.07858"},{"title":"CO adsorption on Cu(111) and Cu(001) surfaces: improving site preference in DFT calculations","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/0408394"},{"title":"arxiv.org","url":"https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2505.12914"},{"title":"First principles study of oxidation resistance of atomically flat Cu(111) surface","url":"https://archive.aps.org/mar/2022/g47/5"},{"title":"s11433 012 4868 5","url":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11433-012-4868-5.pdf"},{"title":"Structural and electronic properties of atomic oxygen adsorption on Cu(111) surface: A first-principles investigation","url":"https://bvbvf1.bib-bvb.de/vufind/PrimoRecord/cdi_chongqing_primary_44769476"},{"title":"par.nsf.gov","url":"https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10320492"},{"title":"P 46 a11 6122919","url":"https://accms.mobility.niche.tohoku.ac.jp/ACCMS-11/POSTER/P-46_a11_6122919.pdf"},{"title":"PhysRevB 73 165424","url":"https://condensed.physics.usyd.edu.au/old_website/papers_aloy/PhysRevB_73_165424.pdf"},{"title":"First principles study of oxidation resistance of atomically flat Cu(111) surface","url":"https://https-meetings--archive-aps-org-443.webvpn1.xju.edu.cn/mar/2022/g47/5/"}]},"The search turned up one clear PBE result and one conflicting value, so the number depends on the source.\n\n- **Best match (PBE, VASP):** An arXiv paper on O on Cu(111) reports the fcc hollow site at -5.000 eV and the hcp hollow at -4.905 eV. The exchange-correlation energy was described using the GGA-based PBE functional. The energy reference is an isolated oxygen atom in the gas phase. In that convention, negative values mean binding is favorable. The fcc and hcp threefold hollow sites are the energetically preferred binding sites for O*.\n- **Conflicting value:** A Tohoku poster reports adsorption energies of -6.37 eV (fcc) and -6.26 eV (hcp) on Cu(111). The calculated adsorption energies were -6.37 eV and -6.26 eV for the fcc and hcp hollow sites, respectively. The poster names a PAW method but does not state the functional, and its reference state is not clear. The gap of about 1.4 eV from the PBE value suggests a different reference or setup, so I would not treat it as a PBE benchmark without checking the original paper.\n- **Other support:** A study of atomic O on Cu(111) also found the fcc-hollow site is the most stable site for oxygen adsorption. That result does not give an energy value or functional.\n\nBottom line: the most direct PBE number for fcc-hollow O on Cu(111), referenced to a gas-phase O atom, is about -5.0 eV. The -6.37 eV figure is probably not comparable without checking its setup."],"durationSeconds":7.676595645000009,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0191U4LzKmntn3GpjvarTuyx","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RKa7EaZVwj5sS6kMxt4sYq","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Xu Mavrikakis 2001 adsorption and dissociation of O2 on Cu(111) binding energy atomic oxygen fcc\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"unibo.it\",\"url\":\"https://acnpsearch.tweb-dev.unibo.it/singlejournalindex/86725655\"},{\"title\":\"scielo.br\",\"url\":\"https://scielo.br/j/mr/a/7JTVH5pDWCwb78HgZ8XXKRG/?lang=en\"},{\"title\":\"par.nsf.gov\",\"url\":\"https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10187796\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2505.12914\"},{\"title\":\"pubs.rsc.org\",\"url\":\"https://pubs.rsc.org/it-it/content/articlepdf/2023/cp/d3cp01215h\"},{\"title\":\"Catalysts (Jun 2024)\",\"url\":\"https://doaj.org/article/a33a72d93b5e494893bbbc9d44468238\"},{\"title\":\"Adsorption and dissociation of O2 on Cu(111):: thermochemistry, reaction barrier and the effect of strain\",\"url\":\"https://data.zhenzhi.com/details/1/867115130\"},{\"title\":\"4.7 Article\",\"url\":\"https://www.peeref.com/works/757048\"},{\"title\":\"Structural and electronic properties of atomic oxygen adsorption on Cu(111) surface: A first-principles investigation\",\"url\":\"https://bvbvf1.bib-bvb.de/vufind/PrimoRecord/cdi_chongqing_primary_44769476\"},{\"title\":\"The adsorption and dissociation of O2 molecular precursors on Cu:: the effect of steps\",\"url\":\"https://data.zhenzhi.com/details/1/888079892\"}]\n\nThe search turned up the 2001 Surface Science paper's abstract and a few follow-up studies. I did not find the full text, so the details below come from abstract-level summaries.\n\n- **Preferred site and binding energy:** Per the abstract reproduced on one aggregator, the authors report that atomic oxygen favors threefold hollow sites, with a binding energy of roughly -4.3 eV relative to a gas-phase O atom (Atomic oxygen is found to prefer threefold hollows. with a binding energy of ca. -4.3 eV with respect to a gas-phase O atom.). The fcc hollow is one of these threefold sites.\n- **Molecular precursors:** The same abstract describes two molecular precursors, both with binding energies of about -0.50 eV relative to gas-phase O₂ (Two types of di-sigma -type chemisorbed molecular precursors are identified: a non-magnetic type located over threefold hollows and a paramagnetic type over bridge sites, both with a binding energy of ca. -0.50 eV with respect to a gas-phase O-2 molecule.).\n- **Dissociation barrier:** The lowest activation energy for dissociation is about 0.20 eV (Possible pathways for the dissociation Of O-2 are explored, and the lowest activation energy is calculated to be ca. 0.20 eV.).\n- **Strain effect:** Expansive strain parallel to the surface strengthens binding of both atomic and molecular oxygen and lowers the dissociation transition-state energy (Expansive strain parallel to the surface plane is shown to enhance the binding of atomic and molecular oxygen on the surface as well as to decrease the transition state energy of O-2 dissociation.).\n- **Cross-check:** A later arXiv study using different methods reports a fcc-site O binding energy of -5.000 eV, with hcp at -4.905 eV (Oxygen (O*) fcc -5.000 hcp -4.905). That value is about 0.7 eV more negative than the 2001 figure, so the two numbers should not be compared directly without checking the reference state and functional used.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e8134b61-5a82-4eb5-8ce2-5d1136f1766d","uuid":"53a9764f-3448-4bd1-81dd-e8710fa5f5b7","timestamp":"2026-11-01T02:52:03.828Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Xu Mavrikakis 2001 adsorption and dissociation of O2 on Cu(111) binding energy atomic oxygen fcc","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_019RiJMfgV1PQ6WWpsbEf2eu","content":[{"title":"unibo.it","url":"https://acnpsearch.tweb-dev.unibo.it/singlejournalindex/86725655"},{"title":"sciel